# ServiceRadar v1.4.15

Fixed anomaly episode payload persistence and silent alert-rule load failures
found during live verification of the v1.4.14 rollout.

## Whats New

**1.4.15**
- Anomaly episodes: episode payloads are stored as JSON objects again — the
  registry double-encoded them, which crashed the device-page anomaly panel;
  a repair migration re-parses rows written before the fix.
- Alert engine: rule-query errors are no longer silently treated as an empty
  rule set. Shards keep their previously loaded rules, log once until
  recovery, emit failure telemetry, and retry — schema drift can no longer
  quietly disable alerting.
- Alert rules: the seeded endpoint inventory vulnerability rule is repaired
  from the retired causal inventory subject to the analytics subject family.

# ServiceRadar v1.4.14

Restored anomaly and capacity alerting operability from edge detection through
the alert engine, user surfaces, and operational tripwires.

## Whats New

**1.4.14**
- Anomaly alerting: production now schedules the seasonal disposition,
  edge-baseline, configuration-projection, stale-episode, and stale-alert
  workers in the deployed core runtime. Stateful alert rules reconcile the
  retired prediction subject prefix, and alert-engine shards no longer land on
  repo-less gateway nodes.
- Detection visibility: anomaly episodes are enabled by default, device pages
  surface CPU findings without a missing disposition record, and stale alert
  cleanup preserves alerts backed by live episodes. New liveness, ingest
  silence, and seasonal-baseline freshness tripwires make regressions visible.
- Capacity forecasting: CPU, interface, and flow sources can be opted in from
  Settings or deployment configuration; the health view reports skipped series
  and uses only persisted forecast statuses.
- Streaming durability: prediction verdicts have a dedicated 24-hour,
  file-backed JetStream stream, with safe fallback handling during the existing
  stream migration. Falco consumption aligns with the provisioned events
  stream.
- Profile safety and release verification: anomaly profile configuration now
  has a database-enforced single-enabled-profile invariant, resolving legacy
  duplicates deterministically at migration time. The anomaly scorecard corpus
  is committed and exercised by the CI floor gate.

# ServiceRadar v1.4.13

Durable plugin-result delivery and deterministic service-state recovery across
rolling upgrades.

## Whats New

**1.4.13**
- Plugin-result delivery: acknowledgement-capable agents retain ordered result
  batches until the gateway confirms `Received=true`, preventing transient core
  or handler failures from silently dropping results. Capability-gated strict
  delivery preserves compatibility while older and newer agents overlap during
  rolling upgrades.
- Deterministic service state: core now surfaces downstream handler failures,
  validates trusted reported-result markers, serializes assignment lifecycle
  and repair work, and uses stable winner ordering for equal-time observations.
  Collision-safe observation slots and a partial identity index prevent
  same-time results from different gateways from corrupting current state.
- Recovery and upgrade safety: plugin-result replay and history repair converge
  state after interrupted processing, and the identity-index migration works
  with both Timescale hypertables and baseline regular PostgreSQL tables.
  `RollingUpdate` remains the supported deployment strategy; raw result history
  stays durable during bounded old/new core overlap.
- Maintainability: the service-state registry and service LiveViews are split
  into focused modules, with the plugin-result regression suite divided into
  targeted fixtures and cases while preserving existing public workflows.
- Build reproducibility: PostgreSQL 18 pgvector is repinned to the official
  verified 0.8.5 package, and the retained-delivery test has its generated
  Bazel dependency registered. (PR #4545)

# ServiceRadar v1.4.12

Endpoint-inventory, flow-attribution, and add-on recovery, with uncapped
threat-intel ingestion and safer control-plane operation.

## Whats New

**1.4.12**
- Endpoint inventory: ScaLibR 0.1.2 now uses the shared
  cache/pending/acknowledgement lifecycle, rejects incomplete scans as current
  state, repairs elapsed systemd timers, and preserves packages and artifacts
  across retries and same-hash freshness updates. Same-agent ingests are
  serialized, endpoint results bypass unrelated router congestion, and the
  freshness contract is consistently 26 hours. Requires base agent 1.4.12.
  (PRs #4535, #4555)
- NetFlow and attribution: database deadlocks no longer cause attributed-flow
  prefixes to be acknowledged and discarded. Delivery now retries ordered
  persistence, removes only positively acknowledged byte-bounded prefixes, and
  quarantines individually oversized records. SRQL application filters and
  partition/protocol rule correlation are corrected, local-CIDR anchors appear
  in flow details, and topology fallback points no longer produce ocean-bound
  map arcs. (PRs #4540, #4546)
- Threat and vulnerability feeds: AlienVault OTX 0.3.3 removes the configured
  maximum-IOC cap and streams bounded pages through durable continuation,
  transactional persistence, and resumable retrohunt state. PowerDNS 0.1.2
  reports a missing Recursor protobuf producer as degraded instead of silently
  appearing healthy. VulnCheck feeds again select encrypted broker-managed
  credentials; installations with no surviving token must supply a replacement.
  (PRs #4537, #4542, #4544)
- Add-on operations: upgraded agents restore delegated cgroup-v2 CPU, memory,
  and process limits; workload-identity 0.1.5 preserves its spool across package
  upgrades; native package sync reuses only exact verified artifacts; and stable
  policy assignment keys stop metadata changes from creating duplicate enabled
  assignments. Agent and fleet views now distinguish assigned, required, and
  unmanaged runtimes without counting required platform services as drift.
  (PRs #4538, #4539, #4543, #4547, #4549)
- Observability and security: fatal-card multi-severity log queries use bounded
  indexed branches instead of timing out over timestamp-only scans. Critical
  and high security findings link to event details, event IPs link to devices or
  a prefilled search, and typed log promotion preserves processor UUIDs and
  arbitrary source IDs without collisions. (PRs #4533, #4548, #4552)
- Data and topology correctness: source-identity conflicts are persisted in
  transactional batches below PostgreSQL's parameter limit; streamed Proxmox
  children reconnect to previously persisted hosts; canonical topology
  collisions merge before endpoint remapping; and structural and telemetry
  graph writes share bounded cross-replica serialization. (PRs #4528, #4529,
  #4530, #4541)
- Runtime and cluster reliability: Ansible watchdog jobs again transition
  pending, launching, and running jobs to unreachable; only the owning core
  replica closes idle camera relays; workload-specific topology constraints
  spread replicated services with `maxSkew: 1`; and the obsolete NATS ingest
  notifier is removed while post-persistence EventWriter notifications remain
  authoritative. Stateful-alert and embedded-JetStream integration gates are
  also isolated for deterministic CI runs. (PRs #4531, #4532, #4536, #4550,
  #4551, #4553)

# ServiceRadar v1.4.11

Production-safe recovery from v1.4.10, with restored plugin execution,
bounded threat-intel ingestion, faster dashboard hydration, and durable
netprobe attribution.

## Whats New

**1.4.11**
- Upgrade safety: repairs installations already affected by the v1.4.10
  database-ownership regression. The pre-upgrade migration uses the direct
  CNPG connection to restore the canonical PgBouncer `user_search` function,
  normalize its ACL, and return it to the database administrator before
  workloads reconnect through the pooler. The health-event migration now
  respects the table owner, Oban is upgraded and validated at schema v14, and
  startup ownership repair no longer claims unrelated public functions.
- AWX and Wasm plugins: first-party manifests now accept JSON Schema array
  constraints such as `minItems`, `maxItems`, and `uniqueItems`. Credential
  broker grants survive their full lifecycle again after the AshPaperTrail
  compatibility update, restoring recurring AWX inventory synchronization and
  other secret-backed plugin checks.
- AlienVault OTX: plugin v0.3.2 abandons unreliable deep full-corpus offsets in
  favor of a resumable 30-day bootstrap watermark and 100-row initial pages.
  Adaptive retries retain their leaf budget without recursive page retention,
  while every pull is capped at 64 upstream requests and five minutes. Partial
  walks persist `modified_since`, upgrades clamp retained numeric settings to
  the target schema, and the Helm guidance documents FQDN-aware egress for the
  rotating OTX CDN.
- Dashboard responsiveness: camera preview startup is asynchronous and no
  longer blocks the dashboard hydration path. Database job scheduling also
  avoids the multi-second failures caused by the stale Oban schema.
- Netprobe reliability: the agent package owns a systemd runtime-directory
  override that preserves add-on IPC sockets across base-agent restarts. The
  updater also detects and repairs a missing live netprobe socket, keeping
  process attribution flowing while older package-managed units are replaced.
  Existing host installations should upgrade the `serviceradar-agent` OS
  package; a binary self-update alone cannot permanently replace old unit and
  updater files.
- Dependency and release checks: aligns Plug 1.20.3 across the Elixir runtimes,
  updates web test coverage for current dependency behavior, and adds focused
  regression coverage for CNPG recovery, OTX retry/cursor bounds, assignment
  upgrades, plugin manifests, dashboard hydration, and agent IPC convergence.

# ServiceRadar v1.4.10

Netprobe flow-attribution recovery, safer eBPF targeting, and agent
configuration-delivery hardening.

## Whats New

**1.4.10**
- NetFlow attribution: active netprobe profiles now default their runtime switch
  on and expose schema-backed settings in the add-on profile UI without losing
  complex JSON configuration. Explicitly disabled profiles remain disabled.
- Agent reliability: configuration responses are serialized across poll,
  control-stream, and enrollment paths; delayed older responses are rejected;
  and visibility bootstrap and attach state apply before systemd
  netprobe activation. Bootstrap or attach failures retry while netprobe stays
  blocked, without preventing unrelated add-ons from reconciling. Core outages,
  credential failures, and unexpected config responses now retain the agent's
  acknowledged last-good configuration instead of delivering an empty config.
- Fleet operations: add-on reconciliation now loads package requirements and
  records platform, base-agent-version, and capability skip reasons. Netprobe
  is assigned only to Linux host agents that advertise network-visibility
  support; LXC and other container deployments no longer enter add-on failure
  loops.
- Release and demo reliability: restored schema-baseline and integration-test
  quality gates, rollback-isolated database tests, per-run Forgejo integration
  databases, and fail-closed protection against destructive test runs on
  non-test databases. Restored build caches now repair missing transitive NIF
  artifacts before compilation. Sandboxed Wasm builds now isolate mutable Go
  and TinyGo state per action so interrupted module or WASI extraction cannot
  poison later release builds. This release also fixes SRQL JSON wildcard
  parsing, preserves Armis attachment metadata, makes `demo/prod-release` the
  sole Argo image source, and prevents release tags from publishing before
  their commit reaches staging. Unresolved live drift remains operator-synced.
- Release publishing: image preflight now verifies that the release and
  `latest` tags match the immutable commit-tag digest, fails closed on registry
  errors, and retries an interrupted remote Bazel publish once before using the
  explicitly enabled local fallback. Post-publish checks cover all 16 image
  repositories and their signatures. Forgejo keeps the release draft until
  package and managed-agent assets, native add-on and Wasm import catalogs, and
  source and image security bundles all verify; finalization then runs off the
  signing pool before the demo release branch can advance.
- Dependency safety: retired the standalone Jetstream package in favor of
  Gnat's maintained JetStream API and upgraded the core runtime's Ash, Decimal,
  Ecto, Req, and related dependencies. The Proxmox Wasm plugins also refresh
  GJSON and Go cryptography, networking, and system dependencies. Only two
  cowlib advisories without a fixed upstream release remain narrowly waived.
- Data correctness: integration credential references are authoritative and
  fail closed instead of falling back to stale legacy plaintext. Concurrent
  same-device inserts that lose the active-IP uniqueness race retry once after
  the winning row becomes visible, while persistent conflicts still surface.
  Health timelines use a database sequence instead of second-resolution
  timestamps for current-state ordering; the one-time ordering migration now
  fails closed under bounded lock and statement deadlines. Stateful alert
  evaluation uses bounded admission with synchronous backpressure when the
  queue is full or unavailable instead of dropping an evaluation. Security
  event persistence also uses one supervised writer, counts in-flight work
  against capacity, and exposes a real flush barrier instead of detached
  database writers. Cluster-wide rate limiting now restores its Horde
  registration after node identity or registry membership changes instead of
  remaining in local-only enforcement.
- Device identity safety: ships the Armis over-merge disposition dormant and
  dry-run-first. Armis polling agent IDs are now observer provenance, so a
  shared poller cannot bypass the disjoint-MAC veto or become discovered-device
  identity. Execute remains runtime-gated, processes ghost records by default,
  requires exact device/source allowlists for live records, rejects faker-backed
  data, and applies each candidate transactionally. Multi-NIC
  splits are not assumed to self-heal and still require operator signoff. Dry
  runs count MAC-less and local-only records outside the bounded candidate
  budget and report a deterministic bounded sample, so unreachable residue is
  visible before signoff. Live execution also requires the integration source
  partition to match the planned MAC-identity partition and rechecks it under
  lock before mutation.

