  `KVService/Put` trace noise from datasvc, and demoted routine
  credential-broker logs from info to debug.

# ServiceRadar v1.4.4

OAuth2 API access repaired end to end, a DB-backed credential-rules surface, richer Device Details, high-resolution sysmon with metric continuity across device merges, proxmox identity consolidation, and assorted plugin and worker hardening.

## Whats New

**1.4.4**
- Fixed OAuth2 client-credentials API access, which never worked end to end:
  `/oauth/token` rejected valid credentials because the `:authenticate` action
  was a plain policy ANDed with the owner-only read policy and ran with no
  actor, so every request was filtered to empty (`invalid_client`); it is now a
  bypass. The token controller now passes its system actor, and the `/api/*`
  bearer resolver accepts the `api` token type (not just browser `access`
  tokens) that the OAuth flow issues, so API clients can list devices and query
  SRQL. Adds regression coverage for the previously untested path.
- The credential-rules settings page is now the single DB-backed surface for
  managing integration credentials: `api_key` auth, camera inventory/stream
  purposes and a controller/static-host field, plus first-class provisioning
  and selection of AWX API tokens — all stored encrypted and resolved through
  the credential broker, never from k8s secrets.
- Device Details lists every discovery source a merged device came from (agent,
  proxmox, awx, sweep, sighting, hypervisor enrichment, …) instead of a single
  canonical view, links a guest to its parent hypervisor, and no longer drops
  the Guests tab on a contended refresh.
- Sysmon metrics honor a high-resolution `sample_interval` (down to a few
  seconds, per-profile, no silent clamp), the CPU/memory/disk charts size their
  buckets to the selected time range with a 1h/6h/24h/7d selector, and a merged
  device shows its full metric history by reading across all of the identities
  it was previously keyed by.
- The proxmox plugin emits a canonical cluster-scoped identity and every
  configured NIC MAC from discovery, so the two ingestion paths reconcile into
  one device and a host/guest merges with its agent-discovered row on a shared
  MAC — without loosening any DIRE merge rule (locally-administered/overlay MACs
  are excluded to avoid collapsing distinct hardware).
- The AlienVault-OTX plugin survives gateway timeouts (adaptive page-splitting
  and backoff on 5xx) and stops re-walking the full pulse corpus every cycle.
- Fixed an `ansible` `RetentionWorker` crash (`No primary action of type :read`
  for `PlaybookRun`) that fired on every install, including those with ansible
  disabled.

# ServiceRadar v1.4.3

Proxmox inventory reliability (streaming, bounded memory, IP-gated devices), an end to config-delivery churn that restarted agent plugins every minute, an operational AWX playbook-run pipeline, and camera relay browser playback fixes.

## Whats New

**1.4.3**
- Proxmox inventory is reliable on real clusters: the plugin streams guests
  per node with bounded memory (256MB cap respected instead of OOMing the
  WASM runtime), emits per-batch observed-at timestamps so partial results
  age correctly, only emits guests as devices when they carry an IP address,
  and de-duplicates hosts across clusters without fusing distinct nodes.
- Config delivery no longer restarts every agent plugin ~once a minute:
  credential-broker grant re-mints are normalized before comparing assignment
  params (a re-minted grant id/timestamp no longer rewrites the assignment
  row every reconcile), config push is version-aware like the poll path,
  volatile per-generation fields are stripped from the version hash (including
  nested ones), the retired download_token_epoch input is gone, and OCSF
  event ids are coerced consistently. Long-running inventory work (Proxmox,
  AWX, camera) can now actually finish instead of being relaunched mid-run.
- The AWX playbook-run pipeline works end to end: the agent routes `awx.*`
  command verbs (ping, list templates/inventories, launch/fetch/cancel job,
  fetch events for jobs) to the awx WASM plugin's run_check entrypoint through
  the existing action-mode plugin machinery — the credential broker grant
  stays host-side and the API token is injected at the host HTTP boundary,
  never entering the WASM module. AWX-synced devices are launchable, the
  dispatch capability gate that blocked launches is gone, the ansible Oban
  queues are configured in the deployed core-elx release, and the run
  watchdog no longer fails every tick on a missing primary read action.
- Git playbook repositories now sync: the first GitCatalogSyncWorker job is
  seeded on repository create/update with a lifecycle backstop sweep (a newly
  registered repository previously sat at `pending` forever), and Ansible
  settings list failures are logged instead of silently rendering as empty
  lists.
- Camera relay browser playback is fixed: the SecurityHeaders before_send
  hook no longer raises on websocket upgrades (every stream connect returned
  500, leaving players stuck at "Relay: Opening" with zero viewers), and a
  relay source's stored gateway is treated as a routing hint rather than a
  hard filter so relays survive gateway pod churn.
- Hosted runtime plan hooks land for managed deployments, and stale Armis
  northbound Oban conflicts are cleaned up so the northbound sync no longer
  wedges on orphaned jobs.

# ServiceRadar v1.4.2

Proxmox and UniFi plugin fixes, network-agnostic identity hardening for multi-cluster Proxmox, provider-aware credential-rule UX, and a deployable AWX/AAP inventory-sync integration.

## Whats New

**1.4.2**
- Proxmox inventory discovery no longer exhausts the plugin's WASM memory on
  large clusters and now captures guest addresses: guests are enumerated per
  node (QEMU/LXC) instead of pulling one cluster-wide response, LXC network
  config IP/MAC and QEMU guest-agent addresses are extracted onto the guest
  devices, an unreachable target yields partial results with a warning instead
  of failing the whole check, a max-guests cap bounds runaway clusters, and the
  insecure runtime path reuses HTTP connections.
- Multi-homed and multi-cluster Proxmox hosts reconcile correctly: intra-Proxmox
  de-duplication now requires a shared strong identity (a common MAC or Proxmox
  host reference) before collapsing same-hostname rows, so two distinct physical
  nodes that merely share a hostname across separate clusters (e.g. `pve02` in
  two clusters) are never fused — honoring the network-agnostic identity rule —
  while genuine multi-homed hosts and integration_id churn still collapse.
- UniFi Protect camera credentials can target a controller whose reachable host
  is not the discovered per-target address: a static controller-host override on
  the credential rule now wins over the SRQL target host.
- The credential-rule form is provider-aware: it hides SSH host-key,
  auto-discovery, and non-camera purpose fields that do not apply to the selected
  provider, filters the secret selector to the rule's provider and auth method,
  supports creating a secret inline in the rule dialog, and scopes the dry-run
  target preview correctly.
- AWX/AAP inventory sync is deployable end to end: the Ansible automation domain
  is migrated, registered controllers materialize a scheduled awx-inventory-sync
  plugin assignment (one per agent, N controllers) carrying an agent-resolved
  credential-broker grant, controller create/update/delete seeds and retires the
  health/catalog/inventory jobs (re-seeded at startup), and a controller can be
  soft-disabled to pause its jobs and retract its assignment without deletion.
  Per-agent reconciliation is scoped so it never disables other agents'
  assignments, and concurrent reconciles serialize on a policy advisory lock.
- Agent config generation no longer writes a credential-resolution audit row on
  every poll: credential material is still resolved to compute the config
  version, but the audit is committed only when a changed config is actually
  delivered to the agent, eliminating steady-state audit-write amplification.

# ServiceRadar v1.4.1

Topology pipeline resilience, add-on lifecycle operability, plugin credential provisioning, interface-metrics chart fix, and the anomaly engine reliability overhaul.

## Whats New

**1.4.1**
- Fixed the outage that emptied the topology graph: mapper topology evidence
  ingestion no longer rejects records that legitimately lack a neighbor port id
  (SNMP-L2 ARP/FDB attachments, UniFi wireless clients, wireguard-derived
  links) — an Ash empty-string cast trap had silently dropped every such record
  since the DIRE bloat-prevention change — and a partial bulk failure no longer
  aborts the whole payload, so accepted evidence always reaches the AGE graph
  with rejected records counted in telemetry instead of vanishing.
- The canonical topology rebuild can no longer self-destruct under evidence
  starvation: when the upsert produces zero edges while mapper evidence exists,
  the stale prune is skipped and a starved health signal raised; a single prune
  pass refuses to delete more than half the canonical graph without an operator
  override; and both topology self-heal mechanisms now escalate loudly on a
  zero-edge outcome instead of logging "completed" forever.
- Switch-to-host attachment edges can now render: MAC-carrying FDB and UniFi
  client neighbors mint deterministic, merge-inert provisional identities
  (flag-gated via SERVICERADAR_TOPOLOGY_ENDPOINT_IDENTITY_PROMOTION, enabled on
  demo), raw IP/MAC pseudo-vertices are no longer written to the graph, and the
  god view surfaces an explicit backbone-empty warning with per-class edge
  counts instead of silently rendering disconnected islands.
- Restored flow attribution and hardened add-on config delivery end to end:
  assignment params are schema-coerced at the delivery choke point (the
  capture_interfaces string that wedged netprobe config apply fleet-wide can no
  longer ship), the netprobe decoder tolerates documented compatibility forms,
  typed config contracts are enforced on every write path with CI tests that
  decode real core-emitted config with the actual agent decoders, and an
  idempotent remediation task fixes any legacy corrupt rows.
- Agents now apply control-stream config per section with explicit
  permanent/transient dispositions and per-section acknowledgements: one failing
  section can no longer block other sections or wedge config version acks, the
  gateway persists acks instead of debug-logging them, core detects and surfaces
  config-wedged agents (health events plus agent-detail and fleet views), and a
  permanently failing add-on config section now shows the add-on as unhealthy
  instead of silently running on stale config.
- Overhauled the add-on fleet and catalog experience: one row per agent and
  add-on with drift rendered as an honest comparison (never "drift: 0.0.0"),
  catalog-only entries separated from fleet status, no horizontal scrolling or
  clipped badges, long diagnostics in an expandable detail, Import All on both
  the add-on and Wasm plugin catalogs is now stateful and idempotent with
  progress and a result summary, and version pickers default to the latest
  approved version with an explicit up-to-date indicator.
- Unblocked camera credential provisioning: the credential rules UI now offers
  api_key authentication, camera purposes, provider presets for UniFi Protect
  and Axis, and inline API-key/username-password secret creation; the proxmox
  WASM plugin's TinyGo config parser now applies the plugin-inputs template
  (api_token, TLS policy, timeouts) so scheduled Proxmox inventory works again;
  credential rule reconciliation reports real counts with per-rule consumer
  visibility and a redacted effective-inputs preview; and plugin assignments
  warn at save time when a credential-materialized field has no matching rule.
- The camera operations panel now distinguishes inventory availability from
  stream operability with the blocking reason spelled out, so a camera can no
  longer read "Available" while every tile says "Agent offline" without
  explanation.
- Fixed device-detail interface traffic charts undercounting by orders of
  magnitude: the panel was re-differentiating already-rated SRQL values,
  collapsing real megabyte-per-second traffic into kilobyte-scale noise and
  nulling roughly half the points; the rate window in SRQL is also now
  partitioned per series key with a guarded rollover modulus.
- Overhauled anomaly engine reliability and Wasm plugins now republish
  automatically on every release tag (matching native add-ons), so plugin fixes
  ship with releases instead of requiring an undocumented manual dispatch.
- Fixed a placeholder-rewriting regression in SRQL's downsample SQL that made
  every rate-aggregated interface chart fail with a bind-parameter error: the
  rewriter now understands string literals, and the downsample regex no longer
  uses a quantifier that could be mistaken for a bind placeholder.
- Fixed the long-standing "device page keeps reloading" bug: device-updated
  broadcasts no longer destructively remount the detail tabs (interfaces,
  flows, logs) on clustered deployments — same-device refreshes keep current
  data rendered and swap in fresh values when they arrive, with broadcasts
  debounced to one refresh per thirty seconds.

# ServiceRadar v1.4.0

A ground-up Settings redesign on a declarative navigation catalog, camera credential auto-provisioning, and the v1.3.11 fix batch.

## Whats New

**1.4.0**
- Rebuilt the entire Settings experience on a single declarative navigation
  catalog that is now the sole source of truth for every settings surface — the
  icon rail, the category topbar, the left view tree, the breadcrumbs, and a
  Ctrl+K command palette all render from it, so adding a settings page is one
  catalog entry and can no longer break the navigation. The information
  architecture collapses to three top-level categories (System, Network
  Services, Edge Ops) with a two-level collapsible tree; the active page is
  resolved by deterministic longest-prefix URI matching rather than hand-typed
  path checks; every page carries contextual status cards wired to the same live
  sources the pages themselves use (and suppressed on pages that render their own
  metrics); the command palette auto-focuses its search box and lists every view
  with a description; the Audit trail truncates long IPv6 addresses and opens a
  keyboard-accessible event detail modal; and the shell is responsive down to
  mobile. With the catalog complete and verified, the transitional "Original UI"
  toggle and the entire legacy settings chrome have been removed — the catalog is
  now the only Settings UI.
- Generalized the Proxmox-only credential-to-plugin materializer to a
  provider-registry model so camera providers (UniFi Protect, Axis) have their
  host injected from an SRQL-targeted credential rule instead of failing with
  "host is required"; the camera WASM plugins now read the per-target host from
  the plugin-inputs envelope, and stream plugins fall back to the relay source
  URL.
- Fixed a cluster of operational issues: the OIDC callback now passes the
  resolved user (not the raw claims map) to the login step so SSO sign-in
  completes; rolled-out agents are no longer mislabeled "already at the desired
  version"; the favorited interface-metrics query no longer breaks on a semicolon
  inside a SQL comment; add-on profile SRQL assignment targets agents rather than
  devices; the Advanced Manual Assignment Override stays open across field
  changes; the ScaLibr endpoint-inventory add-on ships sensible no-touch defaults
  and no longer demands a per-agent id for cohort or profile assignment; and a
  netprobe sidecar health race (marking the sidecar healthy only after flipping
  status to Running) is fixed.

# ServiceRadar v1.3.10

v1.3.9 release-blocker fixes: database migration-version collision, SSO never-auto-provision, and CI signing idempotency.

## Whats New

**1.3.10**
- Fixed a v1.3.9 release blocker where three database migrations were authored
  with the same version number; Ecto refuses to run any migration when versions
  collide, so a fresh v1.3.9 install silently booted on the prior schema and
  broke authentication, metric ingestion, and plugin assignment. The colliding
  migrations are renamed to unique versions.
- Enforced "no local account, no login" for single sign-on: just-in-time SSO
  account provisioning is now gated behind a new `sso_auto_provision` setting
  that defaults to off, so an SSO identity with no pre-existing local account is
  denied rather than silently auto-created (with a clear operator message and a
  settings toggle to opt back in).
- Made release image signing idempotent so a re-run no longer fails when the
  legacy cosign signature tag already exists on an immutability-protected
  registry.

# ServiceRadar v1.3.9

OIDC/Entra single sign-on, SNMP interface-traffic accuracy, and v1.3.8 regression cleanup.

## Whats New

**1.3.9**
- Brought OIDC single sign-on up end to end: SSO versus local login is now
  enforced server-side (closing a gap where the SSO mode only hid the password
  form) with a per-account local-login opt-in and an operator break-glass
  environment switch; the web session cookie is `SameSite=Lax` so the IdP
  callback round-trips; SSO client secrets persist correctly through AshCloak; a
  Microsoft Entra ID settings preset and hardened ID-token verification (JWKS
  refetch on key rotation, `nbf`/`iat`/clock-skew) ship; and `$2y$` bcrypt hashes
  are accepted on login and password change.
- Made SNMP interface-traffic accurate on high-speed links: interfaces exposing
  64-bit HC counters are now polled and their capabilities decoded correctly, the
  SRQL rate query is counter-wrap aware via a persisted counter width (un-hiding
  traffic that previously read near-zero), and the device interface charts drop
  the capacity reference line, auto-scale to the data, and use a responsive grid.
- Cleaned up the v1.3.8 demo regressions: the EventWriter and its
  flow-attribution prune default on when NATS credentials are present; plugin
  WASM is mirrored to datasvc and protected by reference-aware retention; camera
  plugins assign without a spurious required `host`; web-ng dispatches agent
  commands via an RPC fallback after leaving the Horde mesh; and legacy
  plugin-result metrics are tolerated instead of failing the check.
- Stopped self-scheduling Oban workers from churning every 60 seconds (and
  showing "Paused"); gave syslog records their real severity instead of UNKNOWN;
  and kept unconfirmed anomaly findings out of the event stream with
  confirmation-aware severity.
- Fixed several UI issues: local users can change their own password, the profile
  page no longer bounces to the login screen, and long values in the Job
  Scheduler leader card and Event Details no longer overflow their cards.

# ServiceRadar v1.3.8

Anomaly-engine rigor, dependency-security, and demo-CNPG performance release.

## Whats New

**1.3.8**
- Refactored the anomaly engine away from causal-inference framing into an
  honest robust edge detector plus disposition pipeline: de-causal renames, a
  proof harness, a robust median/MAD (Hampel) plus CUSUM edge baseline replacing
  the edge mean/std, a centralized OCSF schema version, and removal of the legacy
  `signals.causal` subjects.
- Upgraded Elixir dependencies for security: gRPC 0.11 to 1.0, gun/cowlib/mint
  CVE fixes, and hackney, with the affected call sites migrated to the gRPC 1.0
  API.
- Resolved the demo CNPG CPU regression by repairing the flow-attribution
  retention prune (a single un-batched DELETE was failing silently against a
  large backlog and had bloated the table to tens of millions of rows; it now
  deletes in bounded batches so the backlog actually clears), skip-guarding
  redundant workload-identity upserts, routing SRQL flow-throughput averages to
  the continuous aggregate instead of a raw scan, and tuning autovacuum and
  fillfactor on the highest-churn tables.
- Fixed a NATS JetStream subject overlap by making the trapd and flowgger
  stream-subject append wildcard-aware, so a `logs.>` stream no longer collides
  with `logs.snmp`/`logs.syslog`.
- Fixed the agent wedging its config version on a permanent section failure, and
  created the endpoint-inventory spool directories with correct ownership and
  permissions.

